Context: this is the Appendix 'De fontibus salientibus' of E260 (Novi Commentarii Petrop. 6, printed 1761). It applies Euler's friction theory to a practical problem: how high a fountain jet rises when water is fed from a reservoir (castellum) through a long straight conduit.

The formula carried over from §106 is v = (g q/(α b) − l)(1 − e^{−αb/g}). Here:
- v is the 'height due to the velocity' of the issuing water;
- q is the head of the reservoir above the orifice;
- b is the conduit length;
- l ≈ 30 ft is the water-barometer height (atmospheric pressure);
- α is the experimental friction coefficient;
- gg is the cross-sectional area of the conduit, so g is a length scale.

On this page Euler proceeds in steps.
1. For a circular pipe of diameter d, gg = πd²/4, so g = (d/2)√π.
2. With α = 1/4540 (his experimental value), g/α = 2270 d√π ≈ 4023 d. He rounds this to g/α = 4000 d.
3. He writes v = (g/(αb))(q − αbl/g)(1 − e^{−αb/g}) and expands 1 − e^{−x} in powers of x. This gives v = (q − x l)·(1 − e^{−x})/x, with x = αb/g = b/(4000 d).
4. §109 turns this into a computational rule. Let n = L/D (length over diameter) and N = (1 − e^{−n/4000})/(n/4000) = 1 − n/(2·4000) + n²/(6·4000²) − ... Then v = Nq − (3/400)Nn feet, since l/4000 = 30/4000 = 3/400. Writing M = (3/400)Nn, the result is v = Nq − M.
5. §110 restates this as a verbal recipe. The next page promises a table of N and M against n, and notes that air resistance on the jet is neglected.

Modern reading: this is a closed-form head-loss law in which the only geometric parameter is the slenderness ratio L/D.
